    },
    {
      "block_id": "page-0920-block-0003",
      "order": 3,
      "text": "Systematic reviews suggest that patient-specific interventions are more likely to enhance \nadherence in patients with serious mental disorders.23 In addition, NICE has reviewed \nthe evidence for adherence over a range of health conditions. They conclude that no \nspecific intervention can be recommended for all patients.\n",
      "bbox": [
        48.03990173339844,
        80.4871826171875,
        422.7799987792969,
        128.20718383789062
      ],
      "text_sha256": "f4dcd447347a491fea219b797d0abbd47568d1e516a2da1f797dbab050cb370b"
    },
    {
      "block_id": "page-0920-block-0004",
      "order": 4,
      "text": "Note that few studies in this area specifically recruited non-adherent patients (the \nrefusal rate in such patients is likely to be high) and the specific barriers to adherence \nare rarely identified. The small effect size seen in many studies may simply be a conse-\nquence of this unfocused approach. Intervention Mapping (IM) framework24 provides \na clear path for recognising determinants of non-adherence and to choose the evidence-\nbased methods in order to change the underlining factors. IM provides a foundation for \ntargeted, patient-centred, and implementable interventions.\n",
      "bbox": [
        48.04290771484375,
        128.4871826171875,
        422.8030090332031,
